.TH SPRIG 1
.SH NAME
sprig \- a garden defended from below, where an entity is a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/sprig
[
.B -t
|
.B -s
|
.B -b
] [
.B -q
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sprig
is a fixed screen where everything comes down at you: a thing on a stalk
working its way toward the garden, and waves of small fast things
dropping out of the air.
You have a spray that goes straight up and a tank that runs out.
Spraying the thing on the stalk from directly underneath pushes it back;
anything else lets it come on.
A pest that reaches the ground eats a plot, and when the plots are gone
so is the garden.
.PP
The whole game is deciding when
.I not
to fire.
Holding the spray down empties the tank, and an empty tank is a second
of nothing at all.
.PP
Arrow keys or
.B wasd
move;
.BR x ,
.B .
or space sprays.
Enter starts again.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SS An entity is a process
This is what the game is in the tree for.
Every pest is a Dis process: it owns its own state, waits on a channel
for the frame, moves itself, and answers on another.
The driver sends the tick once per pest and takes back one answer per
pest, and knows nothing about which process got which tick \- only that
as many came back as went out.
A dead pest still takes its tick and answers, because a wave whose
answers did not match its ticks would leave the driver waiting.
.PP
It was not obvious this was affordable.
Execution here is serialised rather than parallel, so the processes and
their traffic could have cost more than a loop over an array and bought
nothing but shape.
.IR Procbench (1)
settled it before this was written: a process per entity costs four to
sixteen times a loop
.I per entity,
and at these sizes that is charged on a number too small to matter.
So the shape is affordable, and the honest claim for it is that it reads
well, not that it runs fast.
.PP
The one thing the measurement forbade is spawning per entity per frame,
at seven to fourteen microseconds a go.
These are spawned once a wave.
.B -b
reports what a frame of them costs, by wave:
.IP
.EX
   pests     us/frame      us/pest
       6         0.50         0.08
      12         2.00         0.17
      16         2.00         0.13
.EE
.PP
A frame at 60Hz has 16666 microseconds.
.SS The waves are a file
.B /lib/tiles/sprig.waves
is the whole schedule, one wave a line:
.IP
.EX
COUNT KIND SPEED SPREAD
.EE
.PP
.I Count
is how many come at once \- and therefore how many processes the game
starts.
.I Kind
is
.B flit
(weaves from side to side) or
.B borer
(drops straight, fast).
.I Speed
is hundredths of a pixel a frame downward and
.I spread
is how far a flit wanders, in pixels, ignored by borers.
Lines beginning with
.B ;
are comments.
A count above sixty-four is refused rather than attempted, because every
one of them is a process.
.PP
Making the last wave forty deep is editing that file.
Nothing about the waves, the garden or the art is in the program.
.SS Other options
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test and needs no window.
.B -s
plays four seconds and writes the frame to
.B sprig.raw
as 24-bit BGR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/sprig.art
the sheets, the font and the palettes, as tiles(2) commands
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/sprig.chr
the 8x8 tiles of the garden
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/sprig16.chr
the gardener, the pests and the thing on the stalk
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/sprig.map
the garden
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/sprig.waves
what comes, and how much of it
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/sprig.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR procbench (1),
.IR camber (1),
.IR tiles (2)
.PP
.B /doc/games-plan.md
says why this game is in the tree and what the others demonstrate.
.SH BUGS
.B -t
checks that each pest's own process ran once per frame and moved its own
pest, and it is a real check: doing the stepping in the driver instead
fails it.
It presses no key, so the tables that turn a key into an action are
covered by nothing.
.PP
The pests do not notice each other, or you, or the ground until they hit
it.
A process per entity buys the shape of independence, and this game does
not spend it on anything an array could not have done.
.PP
Nothing measures what the processes cost against the same game written
as a loop: the comparison lives in
.IR procbench (1),
without a game around it, and has not been repeated here.
